2. Make `frame_for` return a struct that owns the memory AND a Frame borrowing it (self-referential). Can't easily.

3. Build frames directly via `Frame::from_parts(slots, arena, start, end)` over owned buffers. The guest writes into `mem`; then I can copy the ring+arena bytes out and construct a Frame over owned copies. That's doable: after guest writes, `host.frames()` gives a Frame; copy `slots` (via opcode bytes) and `arena` bytes into owned Vecs, then `Frame::from_parts(&owned_slots, &owned_arena, start, end)`. But I don't know start/end offsets from outside.

